    "case_id": "548",
    "case_summary": "A 76-year-old man with numerous cardiovascular comorbid conditions presented with weeks of worsening renal function leading to hospitalization. Home medications included rosuvastatin, aspirin, prasugrel, sacubitril-valsartan, metoprolol succinate, ezetimibe, furosemide, and ferrous sulfate. Several weeks after starting a new medication, he experienced decreased appetite, weakness, and fatigue, which persisted for several months. The medication was discontinued after approximately 3.5 months of treatment (two weeks before hospitalization). Despite discontinuation, he still experienced poor appetite and profound weakness, needing a wheelchair. He had one fall approximately one week before presentation, during which he was briefly immobilized.\n\nPatient required wheelchair for mobility. No evidence of physical trauma or findings concerning for compartment syndrome was noted. Subsequently developed reduced left ventricular ejection fraction, oliguria, and rapid atrial fibrillation.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:    * Serum creatinine increased to 760.24 µmol/L (8.61 mg/dL)    * Creatine kinase level: 10,698 U/L    * Urinalysis showed myoglobinuria    * Urine sediment showed acute tubular necrosis with muddy brown casts    * Additional findings: hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia, lactic acid increased to 10 mmol/L      - Other Tests:    * ANA: Negative    * Tickborne panel: Negative      - Imaging:    * CT abdomen and pelvis showed no hydronephrosis      Figure Title: Serum creatinine relative to medication initiation/discontinuation",
